1
00:00:00,686 --> 00:00:05,600
And it's my pleasure to have Jumi here from Bloomberg to talk about the last mile of certified programming.

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:06,212
Welcome.

3
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:08,160
Thanks for having me.

4
00:00:08,334 --> 00:00:09,040
Hi, hi everyone.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,100
I'm Jumi Korkut.

6
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:12,160
I'm a researcher at Bloomberg.

7
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:22,560
and today I'm going to talk about certified programming bottles are a flagship project of certified program research program, namely Crane, and particularly about how we're trying to sneak verified programs into Bloomberg software.

8
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,796
But why do we care about verified programming so much?

9
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:30,852
it's mostly because programming in unsafe languages is a never-ending whack-a-mole.

10
00:00:31,214 --> 00:00:38,308
So I'm sure everyone in this room at some point in their lives encountered unexpected overflows, undefined behaviors, seg faults that derailed their programs.

11
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:47,460
And testing, programming defensively, using all kinds of sanitizers, these are all great tools that help tools that help, but they don't actually give you any exhaustive guarantees.

12
00:00:48,046 --> 00:00:51,108
And it's still possible to have these issues pop up in your programs.

13
00:00:51,854 --> 00:00:55,908
And these tools can find bugs, but they're essentially useless for proving their absence.

14
00:00:56,270 --> 00:00:57,604
So we need one verification for that.

15
00:00:59,598 --> 00:01:01,284
But which way?

16
00:01:02,222 --> 00:01:06,468
So one option is to verify existing software with various tool chains.

17
00:01:06,798 --> 00:01:09,636
So I listed some here for C, for Rust, and other languages.

18
00:01:10,318 --> 00:01:16,740
This approach is generally fine, but these tools are often clunky because the languages they operate on have complicated semantics.

19
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:23,972
the proofs can also be brittle, and also they often sacrifice expressivity in the spec language for automation.

20
00:01:25,454 --> 00:01:27,760
So here's an example of what that looks like for a C program.

21
00:01:28,014 --> 00:01:31,300
So here we have a Lemenstein distance function, edit distance function for two strings.

22
00:01:32,014 --> 00:01:35,588
A serious serious implementation of this function is gonna require dynamic programming.

23
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:40,708
So and this is something that you're gonna learn in the first couple of weeks of your algorithms class, probably.

24
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,760
proving this correct is difficult.

25
00:01:44,078 --> 00:01:45,252
So here's a part of this correctness proof.

26
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,840
It's not the whole thing, the whole thing is 4,000 lines of code.

27
00:01:48,206 --> 00:01:50,532
So this one is using the VST framework and the rock proof assistant.

28
00:01:51,022 --> 00:01:56,356
Uh rock uh VST is essentially letting us do separation logic reasoning about C programs using CommStart semantics.

29
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:00,292
there are other tools that you can use to reason about C programs as well.

30
00:02:00,942 --> 00:02:06,880
some of those have more automation, but this is the one that I'm used to, and those tools have other issues, but yeah, anyway.

31
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:13,440
So, an important reason for for this difficulty that it takes this long is that C programming is a never-ending vacuum.

32
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:19,140
Once again, there are a lot of pitfalls that we have to cover in our proof, and without sufficient automation, that takes a long time.

33
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:27,972
And tools that with more automation have less expressivity in the specs, or the automation can take a long time to run, et cetera, So there are other problems with that.

34
00:02:28,494 --> 00:02:37,412
That being said, if we're interested in uh similar but maybe less foundational style of verification, functional translation tools like ANES, for example, are worth looking at.

35
00:02:38,478 --> 00:02:42,340
And also this kind of verification is easier now with AI agents, certainly.

36
00:02:42,606 --> 00:02:45,348
But imagine trying to prove stuff for a much longer function.

37
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:51,012
not to mention we can only do this for for this function because we have a mechanized semantics of C.

38
00:02:52,142 --> 00:02:56,960
so the story is much more complicated when you have to do this for other languages.

39
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:58,788
So let's go back to this.

40
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:00,420
Which way?

41
00:03:01,486 --> 00:03:04,324
I think going from the proof to the implementation is a better way.

42
00:03:05,198 --> 00:03:06,884
So that is often called certified programming.

43
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,988
That is creating new software that is correct by construction.

44
00:03:10,446 --> 00:03:15,140
We implement our program along with the spec and correctness proofs in a language that can express both.

45
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:18,340
And then we can compile or extract that to a lower level language.

46
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:28,868
this style of programming is barely novel, of course, but there are other concerns with its efficiency and readability of the generated code, so those are the parts that I'm trying to attack today.

47
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:35,440
So the main question I had was how can I make certified programming work for Bloomberg's needs?

48
00:03:37,230 --> 00:03:38,852
And my solution for that is Crane.

49
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:45,040
Crane's a new extraction system from ROC, which happens to be my favorite pre-oriented language, to C.

50
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:53,476
Crane generates mostly functional style, memory safe, thread safe, UV-free, and very importantly readable C code from from ROC code.

51
00:03:54,126 --> 00:03:56,708
Sunjoining bytecode, it's not joining C with void pointers.

52
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,228
we want to generate modern C code that engineers can read and audit.

53
00:04:01,038 --> 00:04:04,932
Though I should note it's an active development, so treat these as if there's an asterisk next to them.

54
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:07,200
but it's available open source and it's already usable.

55
00:04:07,406 --> 00:04:08,612
And I'm gonna show you some examples today.

56
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:13,732
I should also note that rock already had extraction to some languages like OCaml and Haskell.

57
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:17,860
we started as a fork of that two years ago and diverged quite a bit at this point.

58
00:04:19,726 --> 00:04:23,204
So here's the workflow that I have in mind for using this in a production setting.

59
00:04:23,886 --> 00:04:25,920
Suppose we have a library written in C.

60
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:28,996
We have tests for our library in a separate file.

61
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:31,520
Now we can use our library.

62
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:35,908
Suppose we have an app that uses our library, and surely our app's gonna have its own tests.

63
00:04:36,366 --> 00:04:40,964
it's nice and all, but we're not protected against all the pitfalls that we mentioned, so we should improve it with Crane.

64
00:04:41,486 --> 00:04:43,460
So we write a spec for our library in the proof assistant.

65
00:04:44,622 --> 00:04:47,396
Uh, and then we implement our library in crane, uh uh in rock.

66
00:04:48,142 --> 00:04:53,604
And then we write a computer check proof in rock that our library implementation satisfies the spec.

67
00:04:54,574 --> 00:04:57,956
And then crane generates a new library in C from our rock implementation.

68
00:04:59,246 --> 00:05:00,996
And then we replace the old library with the new one.

69
00:05:02,510 --> 00:05:05,696
So formal methods is famously expensive and time consuming.

70
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:07,812
How are we gonna get the resources for all this?

71
00:05:08,494 --> 00:05:11,616
Well, formal method is less expensive now with AI.

72
00:05:11,776 --> 00:05:12,516
We all know that.

73
00:05:12,976 --> 00:05:17,856
We can have AI agents write the proof based on the spec and the implementation that we have.

74
00:05:18,286 --> 00:05:24,420
If you're feeling even more adventurous, you can have AI agents write your initial implementation and you can later review and iterate on it.

75
00:05:25,198 --> 00:05:27,296
Could you also have an AI agent write your spec?

76
00:05:27,456 --> 00:05:28,096
Well, yes.

77
00:05:28,270 --> 00:05:33,056
But that's a deeper discussion about the formalization and whether you can trust it, and when we can trust it.

78
00:05:33,294 --> 00:05:38,180
So for now, I'm gonna spec to I'm gonna stick to writing specs by hand and using AI agents to write the proofs.

79
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:41,924
I wanna be more deliberate in what we want our programs to do.

80
00:05:42,510 --> 00:05:45,060
And writing a spec by hand helps with that quite a bit.

81
00:05:45,774 --> 00:05:48,656
And this workflow also allows us to be more realistic about formal verification.

82
00:05:48,846 --> 00:05:50,980
After all, not everything has to be formally verified.

83
00:05:51,310 --> 00:05:53,764
So we verify the critical components.

84
00:05:53,870 --> 00:05:56,932
We verify what we can, but not everything is formally verified.

85
00:05:57,486 --> 00:06:02,884
So we're basically increasing the overall assurance in our programs by verifying the critical components that are shared by many applications.

86
00:06:04,896 --> 00:06:05,856
Well, why C?

87
00:06:06,736 --> 00:06:10,436
Well, first, because C is the primary program language of Bloomberg.

88
00:06:10,576 --> 00:06:11,812
It's it's our lingua franca.

89
00:06:12,336 --> 00:06:14,340
so we're meeting our engineers where they are.

90
00:06:14,976 --> 00:06:21,316
our goal is to generate readable, verified libraries in C for engineers to integrate into their production code.

91
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:29,028
But a deeper reason that we discovered along the way is that modern C has uh has rich overhead uh zero overhead abstractions.

92
00:06:29,966 --> 00:06:36,484
Variants give us tagged unions without virtual dispatch, shared pointer gives us shared ownership without garbage collection.

93
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:46,660
move semantics gives us efficient transfer without copying, concepts give us type constraints, so more compile time safety, constext gives us compile time evaluation for pure functions.

94
00:06:47,022 --> 00:06:53,636
These are all nice features for handwritten code, but they also make C a portable assembly language almost for functional compilers.

95
00:06:53,966 --> 00:06:57,892
And they're expressive enough to generate readable code and efficient enough for production.

96
00:06:58,702 --> 00:07:00,996
So if you take away one thing from this talk, let it be this.

97
00:07:01,646 --> 00:07:09,284
The zero overhead abstractions that C engineers already use, variants, share pointers, et cetera, are a good compilation target for verified functional programs.

98
00:07:09,998 --> 00:07:17,188
And the generated code uses the constructs that a C engineer would reach for if they were writing this by hand.

99
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,056
So let me show you what I mean.

100
00:07:19,758 --> 00:07:21,796
So on the left we have a rock definition of linked lists.

101
00:07:23,182 --> 00:07:24,656
nil and cons are the constructors.

102
00:07:24,750 --> 00:07:26,256
This is just like any other function language.

103
00:07:26,542 --> 00:07:28,576
On the right side, we have Crane's translation of it to C.

104
00:07:29,454 --> 00:07:31,140
So here are the crucial ideas behind this translation.

105
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:36,516
Each constructor becomes a struct holding the constructor arguments, the type becomes a variant that can choose one of those structs.

106
00:07:37,006 --> 00:07:40,976
And parametric polymorphism in rock becomes template polymorphism in C.

107
00:07:41,870 --> 00:07:43,376
And there's no manual memory management.

108
00:07:43,456 --> 00:07:44,964
We just leave that to smart pointers.

109
00:07:45,390 --> 00:07:50,948
It's probably a controversial design choice, but it's also one that we're exploring more for more efficiency.

110
00:07:52,462 --> 00:07:58,656
but in the simplest case, we can do that and we can stay away from maintaining a separate ROC runtime in C.

111
00:07:59,776 --> 00:08:03,748
and we can depend on these zero abstractions to do the memory management for us.

112
00:08:05,998 --> 00:08:06,656
So here's more.

113
00:08:06,816 --> 00:08:08,644
On the left side we have an append function for lists.

114
00:08:09,376 --> 00:08:12,176
this should be prereadable to you if you're familiar with functional programming.

115
00:08:12,336 --> 00:08:14,596
On the right side we have Crane's uh translation.

116
00:08:15,310 --> 00:08:17,856
So notice how append is not a static function.

117
00:08:17,936 --> 00:08:21,444
It's a method that lives in the same struct as our list type definition.

118
00:08:22,318 --> 00:08:27,812
So here it takes two arguments on the left, and on the right side it takes one argument, right?

119
00:08:29,742 --> 00:08:33,443
what happened in the first argument is just this, the object instance.

120
00:08:33,535 --> 00:08:35,812
So it doesn't need the first argument, you don't need a second.

121
00:08:36,366 --> 00:08:40,323
Uh we don't do template quantification separately for the method, so it's just the one outside of struct.

122
00:08:41,616 --> 00:08:46,916
and apart from that, we're just checking the tag of the variance and and if and acting accordingly.

123
00:08:48,110 --> 00:08:54,336
And when we had to create a new list, we have smart constructors that handle smart pointer business for us, so they're also abstracted away.

124
00:08:55,758 --> 00:08:59,776
Here's the map function for lists, and the C crane generates for it.

125
00:09:00,142 --> 00:09:04,336
It's pretty similar to what we have for a append, but now we have more type parameters.

126
00:09:04,686 --> 00:09:06,852
we do another template quantification for the extra ones.

127
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:09,764
Notice how we have quantification outside the struct and inside the struct.

128
00:09:10,496 --> 00:09:11,716
we have higher order functions.

129
00:09:12,078 --> 00:09:14,176
So there are a few ways of doing higher-order functions in C.

130
00:09:14,830 --> 00:09:16,612
Function pointers, std function, et cetera.

131
00:09:16,974 --> 00:09:27,812
These have different upsides and downsides, so I don't want to get into that here, but we get around that by just using the forwarding reference, the double ampersand, then using a template constraint that says the thing that behaves like a higher order function when invoked.

132
00:09:28,398 --> 00:09:29,156
Okay, that's great.

133
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:33,856
Now, does anyone see any problem with this, with these append and map functions that we produce on C?

134
00:09:35,246 --> 00:09:36,740
Well, recursion is the problem.

135
00:09:37,742 --> 00:09:43,936
We have a limited call stack size, and a sufficiently deep chain of recursive calls is gonna fill that, and we're gonna get a stack overflow.

136
00:09:44,016 --> 00:09:44,736
So, what do we do?

137
00:09:45,038 --> 00:09:48,292
We cannot just depend on our runtime like other functional compilers would do.

138
00:09:50,318 --> 00:09:51,312
We convert that into a loop.

139
00:09:51,472 --> 00:09:54,832
So Crane has a pass that we call loopification, currently.

140
00:09:55,118 --> 00:10:01,796
It's not a very good name, but that pass analyzes a rock function and decides what sort of optimization to do.

141
00:10:02,432 --> 00:10:07,332
so loopification is a composition of many different ideas from the functional programming compilers literature.

142
00:10:07,950 --> 00:10:25,284
If I can summarize, depending on what sort of function you have, it does selective CPS transformation at the recursive sites, it does defunctionalization and replicates call frames as explicit objects in the stack data structure, it handles tail calls, it handles tail modular cons, which is a pretty common pattern in functional programming lists, just like in map and append.

143
00:10:26,446 --> 00:10:29,508
But of course the main challenge is to keep the resulting code typable and readable.

144
00:10:29,806 --> 00:10:31,940
So that is the main design constraint that drove our decisions.

145
00:10:33,262 --> 00:10:40,612
So I have a bunch of examples of these that I'm not gonna go super oh yes, super deep into, but just to get a sense.

146
00:10:41,742 --> 00:10:42,692
this is a map function.

147
00:10:43,632 --> 00:10:46,852
so here we choose the tail modular cons optimization.

148
00:10:47,278 --> 00:10:51,268
That means our recursive call is of the form something cons recursive call.

149
00:10:51,694 --> 00:10:55,460
This doesn't have to be cons recursively, it can be any constructor, it can be multiple constructors, whatever.

150
00:10:56,270 --> 00:11:01,392
The Okamu folks came up with optimization that allows you to do this in constant space for function calls.

151
00:11:01,472 --> 00:11:04,992
So here we use the same idea to generate C code that runs the same way.

152
00:11:05,198 --> 00:11:09,220
And it rebuilds the constructors in place with a hole left for the next recursive call.

153
00:11:09,550 --> 00:11:12,432
Except it's it's a loop next loop around, I guess.

154
00:11:12,992 --> 00:11:15,312
Crane can turn tail calls into loops as well.

155
00:11:15,566 --> 00:11:24,580
The main idea is to hold temporary variables for what arguments recursive calls would have, and where you would have recursive calls, you would update those temporary variables and continue to loop.

156
00:11:25,326 --> 00:11:31,972
And even without this optimization, Clang can sometimes optimize tail recursion, but you want to optimize it without having to depend on what Clang chooses.

157
00:11:32,912 --> 00:11:38,596
not to mention, we don't want to force our users to pick higher optimization levels for their code, and so we loopify it ourselves.

158
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:43,792
And the loop is I think pretty readable in C standards at least.

159
00:11:44,814 --> 00:11:45,952
So this is the final one.

160
00:11:46,286 --> 00:11:48,900
If none of them apply, we still have a way of loopifying.

161
00:11:49,392 --> 00:11:52,192
here we have a list length function with in a less than ideal way.

162
00:11:52,718 --> 00:11:55,552
So don't assume that crane just handles length function this way.

163
00:11:55,662 --> 00:11:57,252
This is just a simple example, okay?

164
00:11:57,934 --> 00:11:59,952
And this is why it's less than ideal.

165
00:12:00,352 --> 00:12:08,612
ideally you would keep an accumulator as an argument and do it tailor recursively, or you would do it instead of by one plus, you would do a successor constructor, something like that.

166
00:12:08,752 --> 00:12:13,220
And a smarter compiler might be able to optimize this, but we're not doing that for different reasons.

167
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,284
So I'm gonna use this function as an example, just for frame-based simplification.

168
00:12:18,382 --> 00:12:27,396
So for any function that has to keep things in the call stack, we create actual stack frame data types that hold intermediate data.

169
00:12:28,432 --> 00:12:30,564
we store them in a stack data structure.

170
00:12:30,862 --> 00:12:39,620
So here we start with an empty entry point frame in the stack, and in every loop we pop a frame, we use a frame content, and we push new frames as we need.

171
00:12:40,654 --> 00:12:44,452
So it's the same thing as a call stack, but except it's reified into a stack data structure.

172
00:12:45,152 --> 00:12:48,132
and also the stack size is dynamic now, so I can run into stack overflow issues.

173
00:12:49,678 --> 00:12:51,312
Well, what happened to readability?

174
00:12:52,238 --> 00:12:57,152
well we already write functions like this and maybe with a little less administrative noise than this in real life.

175
00:12:57,486 --> 00:13:05,392
If you're implementing something like that first search in C, you would write it not with recursion, but with a stack data structure and loop until the stack is empty.

176
00:13:05,472 --> 00:13:06,592
So it's actually the same thing.

177
00:13:06,702 --> 00:13:09,060
Maybe this can be simplified a little more, but it's almost the same thing.

178
00:13:09,952 --> 00:13:18,564
And some of the noise here is just unavoidable C stuff, like variants, move semantics, but I'm gonna argue that even in that case that's still somewhat readable.

179
00:13:20,366 --> 00:13:24,260
So there's some other interesting ideas in Crane that I think are worth mentioning here.

180
00:13:24,974 --> 00:13:35,872
for example, we translate rock type classes and modules into C concepts and instances and and modules that satisfy those classes and module types to C structs that satisfy those concepts.

181
00:13:35,982 --> 00:13:38,724
And this is usually checked with a st not usually, it's always checked with a static assert.

182
00:13:39,758 --> 00:13:41,700
And rock has dependent types and C doesn't.

183
00:13:42,672 --> 00:13:47,392
now, first, the preferred way of writing programs in rock is to avoid dependent types.

184
00:13:48,032 --> 00:13:51,652
but also, you use dependent types in your proofs that are separate from the programs.

185
00:13:52,430 --> 00:13:59,472
if you still have to use dependent types, they become the any type in C with runtime cast at boundaries.

186
00:13:59,662 --> 00:14:00,512
So that's that's optional.

187
00:14:00,592 --> 00:14:02,020
You can turn it off for performance.

188
00:14:02,958 --> 00:14:07,268
This is very much like the object.magic, object.t stuff in OCaml for those who know.

189
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:11,812
we take advantage of move semantics whenever possible.

190
00:14:12,686 --> 00:14:15,588
we always remove things at their last use when appropriate.

191
00:14:16,192 --> 00:14:20,932
we have simpler optimizations like inductive types, with all null reconstructors becoming enum classes and whatnot.

192
00:14:21,518 --> 00:14:24,900
And single constructor of inductives and records become flat structs, et cetera.

193
00:14:26,830 --> 00:14:28,192
Okay, so that's a lot of material.

