00:15:15,152 --> 00:15:19,300
also future work is an interface in which you can reason about these custom mappings.

208
00:15:21,070 --> 00:15:27,872
But once again, that requires reasoning about C semantics, so that's a major research project.

209
00:15:28,942 --> 00:15:32,420
So, in the other sense of interacting with the real world, how do we write effective programs?

210
00:15:33,070 --> 00:15:38,884
Because rock is a purely functional language, there's no IO, there's no states, there's no concurrency, just functions manipulating values.

211
00:15:39,822 --> 00:15:44,260
But keep in mind that purely functional languages can do effective computation using monads.

212
00:15:44,814 --> 00:15:48,992
And the crane preferred way of doing this is through through interaction trees, though we don't have to use them.

213
00:15:49,072 --> 00:15:50,820
There are other you can define custom monads.

214
00:15:51,470 --> 00:15:59,524
Interaction trees are a variant of something called free monads, in which you can specify different kinds of effects that appear in your program with more granularity.

215
00:15:59,886 --> 00:16:05,072
it's a rabbit hole that I'm gonna stay out of, but they're essential to organizing effects and reasoning about effects.

216
00:16:06,286 --> 00:16:14,272
And using interaction trees, the users can write effective programs in rock, as you see here, and crane makes sure that they're extracted into idiomatic C.

217
00:16:14,926 --> 00:16:19,312
So if you were doing this with OCaml extraction, you would have had a lot of administrative stuff.

218
00:16:19,472 --> 00:16:24,112
You would have to write an uh an I tree interpreter in OCaml, but you don't have to do that stuff.

219
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:27,952
That stuff is all converted into sequential code in C.

220
00:16:29,230 --> 00:16:31,712
Also notice how the unit type and wrap became a void type in C.

221
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:37,604
And using this method I implemented actual programs.

222
00:16:37,806 --> 00:16:40,356
And by that I mean you know call it codex and i, but yes.

223
00:16:40,912 --> 00:16:43,780
I written SDL bindings for Crane and there's remaining games with it.

224
00:16:44,672 --> 00:16:55,524
this is because Edwin Brady, who developed Idris and white space languages, often talks about how Turing completeness is overrated, and we should do Pac-Mai completeness as a measure of expressivity in languages.

225
00:16:55,712 --> 00:16:57,508
So that was my first target with Pac-Man.

226
00:16:57,838 --> 00:16:59,792
Except this game is called Rockman.

