00:18:27,694 --> 00:18:37,348
And I'm happy with this readability because there's a lot of work behind this to flatten the monadic structure of i trees, interaction trees, and to treat the unit type in ROC as a void type.

249
00:18:37,710 --> 00:18:41,636
But all of that is invisible to the C programmers who might want to read the code.

250
00:18:44,430 --> 00:18:47,972
So at this point, I'm sure some of you are thinking this is fine and dandy, but how does it actually perform?

251
00:18:48,768 --> 00:18:58,852
and that's a tricky question to answer at the moment, because there are three different ways of writing programs in ROC, I guess, and they all will perform differently.

252
00:18:59,406 --> 00:19:05,860
We can write code in a functional style without thinking at all about performance, without thinking about memory user implications of the program.

253
00:19:06,368 --> 00:19:09,156
and this is unfortunately how most rock programs are written today.

254
00:19:09,728 --> 00:19:18,020
a lot of people will try to write things in an interesting style that is baking the proof into the function, and careless use of that will also affect runtime performance.

255
00:19:18,870 --> 00:19:23,088
You can write code in a functional style with performance and memory usage always on your mind.

256
00:19:23,566 --> 00:19:31,812
Rock, unfortunately, doesn't give you a type system with borrowing and ownership like Rust or Mojo to get this uh so it's like tricky to get right.

257
00:19:32,302 --> 00:19:33,028
but it's possible.

258
00:19:34,158 --> 00:19:35,460
Or you can write code in imperative style.

259
00:19:35,758 --> 00:19:48,208
Uh rock doesn't have references by default, so you can you have to express them as effects, and you can configure Crane to extract that into what would have been uh or what would be just mutable variables in C.

260
00:19:49,326 --> 00:19:54,660
And actually there's more on that in a paper that I'm writing with Ian Kranimi these days, who was at our intern over the summer.

261
00:19:55,022 --> 00:20:02,148
He explored local and global mutation using interaction trees and proved that input quick sort written in this way is equivalent to the functional recursive quick sort.

262
00:20:03,054 --> 00:20:04,004
So go back to performance.

263
00:20:04,718 --> 00:20:06,404
I only have numbers for the first style for you today.

