269
00:20:31,008 --> 00:20:35,460
I'm still optimizing Crane, so hopefully by the end of the year we're gonna be that in performance.

270
00:20:35,950 --> 00:20:40,804
But my conjecture is that the problem is that we do memory management through reference counting.

271
00:20:41,422 --> 00:20:48,164
Even with a lot of optimizations like biro inference, allocation stealing, like Perseus, we still spend most of our time in reference counting, so that's a problem.

272
00:20:49,614 --> 00:20:53,808
That being said, this benchmark is trying to be really fair to OCaml extraction.

273
00:20:53,888 --> 00:21:02,148
In both of these in both O'Caml and Crane extraction, we have custom mappings, but only in comparable ways.

274
00:21:02,734 --> 00:21:08,324
So if you have a library that you trust in one of these languages, you can have custom mappings into that and get better performance.

275
00:21:08,814 --> 00:21:12,676
For example, in C, there's a great immutable data structures library called Emera.

276
00:21:13,102 --> 00:21:23,876
Uh I created mappings from rock to data structures, like maps and sets into emerald data structures, and that made the creating version three times faster than the OCaml version.

277
00:21:24,878 --> 00:21:27,876
but I'm not in the business of unfair benchmarks, so I'm showing you these.

